{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterclass in modern storytelling, blending sharp writing, relentless pacing, and a haunting score into a compelling portrait of ambition, betrayal, and the digital age. directed by david fincher and written by aaron sorkin, the film doesn’t just chronicle the founding of facebook—it dissects the emotional and moral cost of innovation in a world obsessed with connection and control.\n\njesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him not as a villain or a hero, but as a brilliant, socially awkward prodigy whose isolation fuels his drive. his performance is icy, rapid-fire, and unnervingly precise, mirroring the algorithmic logic of the platform he builds. the supporting cast is equally formidable: andrew garfield as the loyal but betrayed justin portman, and jesse plemons as the quiet, conflicted dupont brothers, both bring depth to characters who are more than just plot devices.\n\nsorkin’s script is a marvel of dialogue—witty, dense, and charged with subtext. every line feels like a legal brief, a psychological probe, or a verbal punch. the courtroom sequences, framed as dual narratives, serve not just as exposition but as emotional counterpoints, revealing how truth is shaped by perspective and power. the film never takes sides; instead, it asks the audience to sit with the discomfort of ambiguity.\n\ndavid fincher’s direction is meticulous, using cold blues and grays to evoke the sterile glow of computer screens and the emotional chill of modern isolation. the cinematography is sleek and unflinching, with every frame feeling calculated, much like facebook’s own design. trent reznor and atticus ross’s score is a revelation—electronic, pulsing, and melancholic, it becomes the heartbeat of the film, a digital lullaby for a generation wired but emotionally disconnected.\n\nwhat makes the social network enduring isn’t its subject matter—it’s how it captures the paradox of our time: we are more connected than ever, yet more alone. it’s a tragedy dressed in hoodie and code, a modern myth about friendship, jealousy, and the price of genius. it doesn’t glorify facebook; it interrogates the culture that built it and the people who were left behind.\n\nby the end, you’re not sure whether to admire zuckerberg or pity him. and that’s the point. the social network isn’t just a movie about a website—it’s a mirror. and like facebook itself, it’s hard to look away.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00012754580529872328}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -2.0265558760002023e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -1.6689286894688848e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0004889961564913392}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.31129345297813416}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -1.2248505353927612}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": " modern", "logprob": -1.288470983505249}, {"token": " storytelling", "logprob": -0.8218672275543213}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0002172949316445738}, {"token": " blending", "logprob": -0.3556341826915741}, {"token": " sharp", "logprob": -0.045483238995075226}, {"token": " writing", "logprob": -0.5288774371147156}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.0847986231965479e-05}, {"token": " relentless", "logprob": -1.7472233772277832}, {"token": " pacing", "logprob": -0.0026524621061980724}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.5766553282737732}, {"token": " haunting", "logprob": -0.049994200468063354}, {"token": " score", "logprob": -0.2472931146621704}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.47408610582351685}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.005887310486286879}, {"token": " compelling", "logprob": -0.7165380716323853}, {"token": " portrait", "logprob": -0.1